    "member_id": "plans-publish-boundary-path-validation-20261108"
  },
  "context": "Publisher bypass and SQL behavior reported by publication batch leader; reproduce at current head before implementing. New hosted data removal or production publication needs separate approval.\n\nDECISIONS (owner, 2026-11-08): detector = known local roots (/Users, /home, /tmp, /var/folders, /private, /mnt, /opt, any Windows drive letter, UNC) PLUS the run's configured data and output directories, with an exact-rule placeholder allowlist (<redacted> and bare /data-style neutral placeholders); NOT every absolute path. Publish boundary = sanitize with the export anonymizer, re-scan, publish only if clean, otherwise reject with a redacted diagnostic and publish nothing (fail closed). ORDER: after the detector item; reuse the export sanitizer from the export item rather than a second sanitizer. GUARDRAILS: stored fingerprints are never recomputed or altered by sanitizing text; plan structure, errors, fingerprint_varied/fingerprint_variants and keys are preserved; never mutate inputs in place; diagnostics must not print the leaked path values; no hosted data deletion, republish or history rewrite; private (non-anonymized) local exports keep their current behavior; false positives are bounded by an allowlist test that real plan text (catalog names, URLs, relative table tails, Spark/LakeSail scans) is not flagged. Verification rubric: (1) reproduce the defect with a failing test or script before editing; (2) focused suites pass via uv run -- python -m pytest -n 0; (3) make comment-policy-check and ruff on touched files; (4) the runtime smoke named in acceptance is recorded in the PR body; (5) one final batch PR merges with green CI and a passing oracle review.",
  "description": "Publication investigation reported benchbox publish run copies companion files byte-for-byte without a path check. Capture-time cleanup cannot protect older or externally produced companions. SQL plans also retain local paths in raw_explain_output when exported without anonymization; a test currently treats that private-export behavior as intentional. The public boundary must not mistake such files for publication-safe artifacts.",
